\section{Experimental design}
\label{sec:design}

\paragraph{Graphs and designs.}
Synthetic graphs have $n=3000$ units and target mean degree $d\in\{5,20,80\}$: a stochastic block model (SBM; blocks of 100, 30\% of edges between blocks), Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi, Barab\'asi--Albert, Watts--Strogatz and a degree-corrected SBM (log-normal propensities) \citep{holland1983stochastic,erdos1959random,barabasi1999emergence,watts1998collective,karrer2011stochastic}. The SBM is the core family, with an $n$ slice ($10^3,3\times10^3,10^4$). The two real graphs are socfb-Stanford3 \citep{traud2012social,rossi2015network} and ego-Facebook \citep{mcauley2012learning}, reduced to the giant component and thinned by independent edge removal to a target degree; thinning also changes clustering and the degree tail, so a degree effect on real graphs is not attributed to degree alone (ego-Facebook at $d=80$ is unthinned, mean degree 43.7). Assignment is Bernoulli($p$), $p\in\{0.1,0.25,0.5,0.75,0.9\}$ (the core grid uses $\{0.25,0.5,0.75\}$), or graph-cluster randomisation with Louvain clusters \citep{blondel2008fast} at resolution $\gamma\in\{0.3,1,3\}$ or the planted SBM partition and cluster-level Bernoulli($p$) draws. Louvain at $\gamma=0.3$ returns one cluster on every synthetic graph, so cluster designs there use $\gamma=1,3$ and the planted partition.

\paragraph{Mechanisms and calibration.}
Outcomes are $Y_i=a_i+h_i(z)+\varepsilon_i$ with unit baselines $a_i\sim N(0,1)$ (in some cells correlated with log-degree or community) and $h_i=(1-s)z_i+s\,\psi_i(z)$, where the exposure response $\psi_i$ is, with $f_i$ the treated-neighbour fraction and $c_i$ the count: (a) $f_i$; (b) $c_i/\bar d$; (c) saturating, $(1-e^{-3f_i})/(1-e^{-3})$; (d) threshold, $\mathbf 1[f_i\ge0.6]$; (e) 30-step diffusion; (f) $\tfrac12(f_i+z_if_i)$, an own-treatment$\times$exposure interaction (Table~\ref{tab:mech}, Appendix~\ref{app:grid}); (g), a convex mirror of (c), is exploratory. Every mechanism is calibrated to $\gate=1$ with spillover share $s\in\{0.25,0.5,0.75\}$ (fine grid $0.1,\dots,0.9$ at the core cell); $\gate$ is computed exactly by evaluating $h$ at $\mathbf 1$ and $\mathbf 0$ on each graph draw. Errors are $(\hgate-\gate)/\gate$. Noise $\varepsilon_i$ is iid Gaussian with standard deviation $\sigma$ times the standard deviation of the noise-free outcome ($\sigma\in\{0.25,0.5,1\}$ planned; $\sigma\in\{2,4\}$ added after the freeze and labelled as such). Under Bernoulli assignment DM's bias is $-s\gate$ by construction.

\paragraph{Estimators and tiers.}
Comparisons are made within an information tier.
\emph{Tier G} (graph known, no mechanism parameter): DM, H\'ajek ($q=0.75,1$), the reporting regression and its ladder (quadratic and spline in $f$, $+$count, $+$two-hop, $+zf$).
\emph{Tier A} (data-adaptive; Bernoulli designs, 14 core and real-graph cells, 200 draws per graph): lasso \citep{tibshirani1996regression} and ridge \citep{hoerl1970ridge} on 15 exposure features, with the penalty chosen by graph-cluster-fold cross-validation and a held-out-corner loss in the spirit of \citet{han2023model}; random forest \citep{breiman2001random} and gradient boosting \citep{friedman2001greedy} from scikit-learn \citep{pedregosa2011scikit}; and a simplified version of the adaptive-threshold H\'ajek estimator of \citet{thiyageswaran2024data}, reported under that name.
\emph{Tier O} (oracle): the correct feature with the true parameter, or with the parameter fitted by profile least squares for (c), (d), (e).
Causal message passing \citep{shirani2024causal} needs panel outcomes, and graph neural networks \citep{kipf2017semi,defferrard2016convolutional} and cluster Horvitz--Thompson are not run.

